---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

// The one page that walks the whole loop, from an empty window to a finished
// JPEG. Labels and shortcuts here are the ones the app itself shows: verify
// against the in-app help (?) before changing them.
const steps = [
  { id: "open", label: "Open the app" },
  { id: "folder", label: "Pick a folder" },
  { id: "browse", label: "Browse the folder" },
  { id: "select", label: "Select a photo" },
  { id: "adjust", label: "Adjust the settings" },
  { id: "export", label: "Export" },
];
---
<Base
  title="Getting started — LightPhotos Help"
  desc="Open the app, pick a folder, browse through it, select a photo, adjust the settings and export a finished JPEG — the LightPhotos walkthrough."
  active="docs"
>
  <main class="lp-page">
    <section class="lp-hero lp-hero-compact">
      <div class="lp-glow"></div>
      <h1 class="lp-wordmark">Get<span class="lp-light">ting started</span></h1>
      <p class="lp-lede">
        Six steps from an empty window to a finished JPEG. There is no import
        step, no account, and nothing ever leaves your machine.
      </p>
    </section>

    <article class="lp-article">
      <p>
        LightPhotos works on a folder that is already sitting on your disk, so
        the whole loop happens in one window: open the app, pick a folder,
        browse it, choose a photo, adjust it, export it. Your ratings and edits
        are written as you go, and you can stop at any point.
      </p>
      <p>
        Shortcuts are written the macOS way &mdash; on Windows and Linux, read
        <kbd>Cmd</kbd> as <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>. Press <kbd>?</kbd> inside the app
        for the full list.
      </p>

      <nav class="lp-doc-toc" aria-label="On this page">
        {steps.map((s, i) => (
          <a href={`#${s.id}`}>{i + 1}. {s.label}</a>
        ))}
      </nav>

      <h2 id="open">1. Open the app</h2>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>In a browser.</strong>
          <a href="/app.html">Open the web app</a> &mdash; nothing to install
          and nothing to update. It renders on the GPU, so Chrome or any
          browser with WebGPU enabled will run it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>As a desktop app.</strong>
          <a href="/downloads.html">Download it</a> for macOS, Windows or
          Linux. It runs with no internet at all once it is on your machine.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Either way it is the same editor over the same files. Photos are read
        straight from disk and never uploaded.
      </p>

      <h2 id="folder">2. Pick a folder</h2>
      <p>
        An empty window has one button: <strong>Choose Folder</strong>. Pick
        the folder holding the shoot you want to work on. LightPhotos scans it
        and fills the grid right away &mdash; Sony ARW, Canon CR2 / CR3, Adobe
        DNG, JPEG, HEIC and TIFF all sit side by side, and there is no import
        step to sit through.
      </p>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>The web build asks for permission.</strong> Your browser
          wants confirmation before any page may read a folder. Grant it for
          the folder you picked; that is the only access LightPhotos gets.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Switch folders whenever you like.</strong> Click
          <strong>Open</strong> in the header, or press <kbd>Cmd</kbd>+<kbd>O</kbd>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Your settings live next to your photos.</strong> Ratings,
          labels and edits are written into a <code>.lightphotos</code> folder
          inside the folder you opened. Move the folder and the edits travel
          with it; delete that folder and you lose the edits, never the photos.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="browse">3. Browse the folder</h2>
      <p>
        The library is a grid of thumbnails, decoded as they scroll into view,
        so a folder of several thousand photos is browsable in seconds instead
        of loading everything up front.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Rate as you go.</strong> With a photo highlighted, press
        <kbd>0</kbd> to <kbd>5</kbd> to set the star rating,
        <kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>1</kbd> through <kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>5</kbd>
        for a color label (red, yellow, green, blue, purple), and
        <kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>0</kbd> to clear it. The Rating and Label tabs
        on the right do the same with the mouse.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Narrow the grid.</strong> The filter row above the grid offers
        a rating filter (all, at least / exactly / at most N stars, unrated),
        <strong>Bursts</strong> (<kbd>B</kbd>), which groups a burst and badges
        the sharpest frame, <strong>Duplicates</strong> (<kbd>D</kbd>), which
        groups visually-similar frames, and <strong>Eyes closed</strong>, which
        needs Bursts or Duplicates switched on first.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Build a selection.</strong> Click to select,
        <kbd>Shift</kbd>+click to take a range,
        <kbd>Cmd</kbd>+click to toggle a single photo, and
        <kbd>Cmd</kbd>+<kbd>A</kbd> to take the whole folder. The arrow keys
        move the highlight; <kbd>Shift</kbd>+arrows extend the selection.
      </p>

      <h2 id="select">4. Select a photo</h2>
      <p>
        Highlight a photo and press <kbd>Enter</kbd> or <kbd>Space</kbd> to
        open it full size in the editor. From there:
      </p>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <kbd>Left</kbd> / <kbd>Right</kbd> step to the previous or next
          photo without leaving the editor.
        </li>
        <li>
          <kbd>Esc</kbd> goes back to the grid; <kbd>E</kbd> and <kbd>G</kbd>
          jump between the editor and the library.
        </li>
        <li>
          <kbd>Cmd</kbd>+<kbd>0</kbd> fits the photo to the window,
          <kbd>Cmd</kbd>+<kbd>1</kbd> shows it at 1:1, and
          <kbd>Cmd</kbd>+<kbd>=</kbd> / <kbd>Cmd</kbd>+<kbd>-</kbd> zoom in
          and out. Hold <kbd>Space</kbd> and drag to pan.
        </li>
        <li>
          <kbd>C</kbd> and <kbd>Y</kbd> flip between the original and your
          edit.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="adjust">5. Adjust the settings</h2>
      <p>
        The panel on the right carries the Rating, Label, Adjustments, Touch Up
        and Rotation tabs, and almost everything happens under
        <strong>Adjustments</strong>:
      </p>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>White Balance</strong> &mdash; Temp and Tint, or click
          <strong>Pick Gray</strong> and then click a neutral-gray pixel in the
          photo to balance from it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Tone</strong> &mdash; Exposure, Contrast, Highlights,
          Shadows, Whites and Blacks.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Presence</strong> &mdash; Vibrance and Saturation.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Detail</strong> &mdash; Denoise. Crop sits alongside these
          sliders, and the Rotation tab turns the photo in 90&deg; steps.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>Three habits make this much faster:</p>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Let the histogram start.</strong> <strong>Auto</strong> sets
          the tone sliders from the photo's own histogram &mdash;
          <kbd>Cmd</kbd>+<kbd>U</kbd> for the photo in front of you,
          <kbd>Cmd</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>U</kbd> for everything
          selected. <strong>Reset</strong> puts the sliders back to zero.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Do one, then copy it.</strong>
          <kbd>Cmd</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>C</kbd> copies the develop
          settings off a finished photo; select the rest of the shoot and press
          <kbd>Cmd</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Y</kbd> to apply them to the
          whole selection.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Heal the small stuff.</strong> The Touch Up tab removes
          spots &mdash; click a blemish and adjust with the Size slider, up to
          64 spots per photo. It needs the full-resolution image loaded first.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        None of this touches the original file. Every slider is an instruction
        stored in the <code>.lightphotos</code> folder beside your photo, so
        you can close the app mid-edit, come back next week, or reset the whole
        photo without any harm done.
      </p>

      <h2 id="export">6. Export</h2>
      <p>
        Select one photo or a hundred, click <strong>Export JPG</strong> (or
        press <kbd>X</kbd>) and confirm. LightPhotos bakes your adjustments
        into brand-new JPEGs and writes them into an <code>Exports</code>
        folder inside the folder you opened &mdash; created the first time you
        export.
      </p>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          The originals are left exactly as they were and the edits stay in
          place, so you can change a slider tomorrow and export again.
        </li>
        <li>
          Exports are ordinary files: open them in anything, attach them to an
          email, hand them to a print shop.
        </li>
        <li>
          Nothing is uploaded. The whole loop above happens on your own
          machine.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </article>

    <section class="lp-actions" aria-label="Keep exploring">
      <a class="lp-card" href="/downloads.html">
        <i class="ph-bold ph-download-simple" aria-hidden="true"></i>
        <h2>Downloads <span class="lp-go" aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span></h2>
        <p>The desktop app for macOS, Windows and Linux, offline and under 10 MB.</p>
      </a>

      <a class="lp-card" href="/blogs.html">
        <i class="ph-bold ph-pencil-simple" aria-hidden="true"></i>
        <h2>Blogs <span class="lp-go" aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span></h2>
        <p>Write-ups on the hard parts: WebAssembly, threading, RAW decoding and color pipelines.</p>
      </a>
    </section>
  </main>
</Base>
